<!--
    The microphone, and the long shadow it casts.

    Both live on the foreground layer so they move together under a launcher's parallax - a shadow
    that slid independently of the thing casting it would be worse than no shadow.

    The shadow is generated rather than drawn by hand. Each part of the glyph is convex, and for a
    convex shape the swept region along the light direction is exactly the hull of the shape and its
    translate; the union of those sweeps is the union's sweep. Taking one hull around the whole
    glyph instead would bridge the gap between the capsule and the yoke and cast shadow through a
    hole the glyph does not block. The pieces overlap freely, which costs nothing at a single opaque
    colour.

    The yoke is one smooth outline here even though the shadow decomposes it into quads: drawing
    those quads put visible facets and hairline seams along the arc.

    Every part of the glyph sits within a radius of 33 from centre, inside the 66dp circle every
    launcher mask keeps. The shadow deliberately runs past that and is clipped by the mask, which is
    what makes it read as continuing off the edge.
-->
